ty for me to introduce another  way that we can improve upon ion  mobility resolution when we talk about  mass spectrometry we're always talking  about the analysis of ions now these  might be protonated or deprotonated ions  or might add on something else like a  sodium ion a phosphate ion or  potentially something else like an  alkali metal  now why does that matter for ion  mobility with what the mass spectrum  what we'll see is simply a difference in  the mass potentially better separation  in chromatography or potentially an  improvement in the signal intensity  within the mass spectrum with ion  mobility when we start to look at  different ions such as in this case  Saudi ated ions we start to see an  improvement in their resolution so here  I would like to show an hi in mobility  plot that includes 25 hydroxy d3 as well  as its epimer and I've included 25  hydroxy vitamin d2 this is the plant  derived source of this molecule and what  we see here is that well the resolution  is slightly better but still not great  but being good analytical chemists we  wanted to look into the data a little  bit more and what we found interestingly  enough was that 25 hydroxy vitamin d3  also has this unique drift peak out here  at around 30 milliseconds now  interestingly enough this same peak is  not seen for the epimer and it's also  seen at a much later drift time  indicating that it's a much larger  structure but what is that well in  trying to figure out exactly what that  compound was with a considerably larger  cross section we wanted to turn first to  our mass spectra and make sure that what  we were looking at was indeed the same  compound or some kind of isomer here and  what we found was that in addition to  this drift peak that we have here at 30  milliseconds we also have a couple of  longer drift Peaks out between 35 and 40  milliseconds now thankfully mass  spectrometry allows us to answer one of  those questions those Peaks out at sea  are actually divers that we can see at  mass-to-charge 823 but the mass spectra  between a and B look nearly identical so  what is that new compound that we're  identifying at a later drift time  well we're going to turn to theoretical  modeling and this is a powerful  combination often used with ion mobility  to get a better sense for what kind of  structural differences are really  contributing to some potential  separations now when we think about ions  in mass spec we're really just making  sure that we can indeed ionize compounds  to begin with and so if we add something  like a sodium ion and we see that in our  mass spectra we're pretty happy and we  don't necessarily think about the actual  physical placement of that sodium ion  within the molecule but what we see is  25 hydroxy vitamin d3 is essentially a  hydrocarbon say for the presence of a  hydroxyl group at each end of the  molecule and so when we add that sodium  ion in with its positive charge it's  most likely going to interact with one  or the other hydroxyl group based on the  partial negative on the oxygen atom but  there's also another potential  opportunity here and that is that based  on the structural flexibility of this  molecule we can actually have this  wraparound such that both hydroxyl  groups are interacting with the sodium  ion simultaneously and what we see here  is when we actually do theoretical  modeling to calculate the lowest energy  conformation of these molecules we see  that indeed they do adopt this closed  low-energy conformation and the  agreement between our theoretical  collision cross-section our experimental  collision cross-section is quite good  however what we also find is that  there's another local minimum another  potentially accessible structure here in  open conformation in which that sodium  ion interacts with only one of the  hydroxyl groups now if we actually plot  out all of our putative structures from  our theoretical modeling and plot them  against the difference in those two  different structures that the closed  conformation versus the open  conformation we see that for Hydrox is  at 25 hydroxy vitamin d3 the difference  in energy is quite small meaning that  both of those confirmations could be  adopted however when we look at the same  kind of energy diagram for our our  epimer we see that that energy diagram  is considerably larger  that open conformation is actually not  energetically accessible and so this  peak that appears here labeled B is our  open conformation of 25 hydroxy vitamin  d3 that allows us to resolve these two  compounds when otherwise we might not be  able to  so let's move into one last example of  the omics moving from very small  molecule metabolites up to very large  molecules like proteomics and the way  that I and mobility can be used to  improve upon that now I've shown an  example of a protein hear retinol  binding protein 1 and if we were to  analyze that using mass spectrometry our  approaches would largely fall into two  different categories the first is what  we would call top-down proteomics this  is the analysis of impact or native  proteins and it can be very challenging  experimentally it generally requires  very high resolution mass spec and a lot  of expertise in this kind of field but  historically easy easier way and the  more commonly used way to identify  proteins is to use what's called  bottom-up proteomics and what this means  is that we actually cleave these  proteins using something like a trypsin  digest and we cut this up into smaller  peptides that we can then analyze using  anything ranging from a very high  resolution mass spectrometer even to a  triple quad instrument for targeted  applications and because this has a less  of an instrumental burden it's a more  common method but where can I in  mobility fit in for this well let's take  an example of a peptide that I've shown  here the mass the charge of 9.99 and  I've highlighted two different serine  residues and that's because Furion is a  common location where we can see a post  translational modification or a PTM  called phosphorylation and we can  potentially phosphorylate either of  those two serine residues now imagine if  we have a mixture of those two different  peptides  or to try to separate them using even  ultra high pressure liquid  chromatography we would see that they  unfortunately are coalition because  there are also isomers we can't  differentiate between them using mass  spectrometry either but when we analyze  that mixture using ultra high-resolution  ion mobility we show here that we have  an arrival time distribution with two  different Peaks not quite baseline  results but good enough to tell the  difference between these two and when we  then run those two compounds as  individual standards we can actually see  that we are able to separate these  isomers based on the placement of that  sauce for relation group whether it's  that syrian towards the the end terminus  or whether it's at that serine in the  middle of that peptide  now when we use this to look at  something like a global protein dry it  digest we're able to get an increased  number of features that we can identify  because we're now taking and separating  in three different dimensions first our  chromatographic retention time and our  ion mobility drift time and lastly our  mass spectrometry mass-to-charge value  and we can see here this cool plot where  we're actually showing the number of  features that are identified in three  dimensions  now one final thing that I want to talk  about is some of the ways that we can  use ion mobility with other cool  innovations to actually get some more  information about impact proteins and  here we have a method that's called  collision induced unfolding what this  allows us to do is take native proteins  that are in their most common folded  state and subject them to increased  energy now as these proteins increase in  energy we'll actually get a gas phase  unfolding of these changing not their  Maps but changing their structure and  when this structure changes we'll see  that as a corresponding increase in  drift time we can then plot that  collision voltage the energy that we're  giving to these proteins as are  unfolding relative to their drift time  so that we can see these characteristic  unfolding events and this tells us a lot  about the dynamics of the protein  folding as well as the overall structure  of those proteins  now there are a lot of new directions in  ion mobility analysis and this is really  where we're moving to improve the way  that we use this in the omics one  particular example is things like higher  resolution instrumentation we've vendors  have introduced things like trapped ion  mobility as well as a more recent Advent  structures for lossless ion  manipulations slim in which we're  actually able to get considerable  increases in resolution between  structurally similar compounds now one  of the things that we'd like to look for  in the future is being able to couple  ion mobility with higher resolution mass  spectrometers not just time-of-flight  instruments this might be things like  orbit Ray's orbit trap based platforms  or potentially even FTA CRM platforms  we'd like to see more continuity in  measurements and calibration as well as  population of CCF databases across the  wide range of vendor options as well as  the different flavors of ion mobility  that I mentioned very early on to be  able to take something with a static  field a constant drift tube based  measurement and compare that CCS to  something like a traveling based  measurement that that's a very powerful  continuity there finally we'd like to be  able to expand upon using other tandem  techniques tandem ms with all iron  fragmentation even looking at things  like spectroscopic techniques where  there's work going on with coupling of  ion mobility with UV and/or IR  spectroscopy as well as being able to do  some gas phase chemistry like ion ion  and iron molecule reactions finally we'd  like to look at some ways that I a  mobility can improve upon some of our  commonly used sampling techniques these  include things like mass spectrometric  imaging as well as ambient ionization  including things like desorption or desi  electrospray ionisation potentially  other analytical areas we'd love to hear  about other ways that I a mobility might  be able to improve upon your analyses  because the well there are since there's  no limit on the kinds of applications  that this could be used for  so finally I'd like to finish by  acknowledging some of my past and  current co-workers including those  listed here at the University of Florida  those listed here at Pacific Northwest  National Lab dr. Aaron Baker as well as  my group here at Florida Institute of  Technology including my graduate student  Aurora burkas Matosevic and several  other students in my group listed here  and with that I'd like to again thank  adjoint for sponsoring this and at this  point I would be happy to take any  questions that you might have  thank you Chris for your presentation a  quick reminder for our audience on how  to submit questions simply type them  into the drop down box located on the  far left of the presentation window  labeled ask a question and click on the  send button  Christel answers many questions as we  have time for our right first question  is what are some of the major  instrumentation advances that can be  expected  well I think I hit upon that a little  bit in my last slide and I think really  what this is is kind of a two-fold  approach one is taking ion mobility to  higher resolution so that when we have  compounds that are very very similar in  collision cross-section much like a lot  of those examples I showed where we  clearly have two different peaks for two  different isomers but are not quite  baseline resolved what kind of  instrumental approaches can we take to  improve upon that and it's it's really a  matter of how high can we get in  resolution just like we're always  pushing the the boundaries of how high  we can go with resolving power and mass  spectrometry we look to do the same  thing with developing higher resolution  ion mobility techniques and like I said  there are a few notable examples of that  that are being developed both  commercially and also in research labs  such as the structures for lossless ion  mobile manipulations platform that's  being developed at Pacific Northwest  National Lab and I think that's going to  really allow us to get better  separations than we can with more  historic instrumentation the second  thing that I would say in terms of  instrumental advances is coupling this  with techniques that allow us to get a  little bit better information about our  molecules and this could be things like  other kinds of fragmentation methods in  addition to just collision induced  dissociation I'm talking about things  like electron based methods electron  capture Association and electron  transfer dissociation as well as some  light based methods like UV photo  dissociation and infrared multiple photo  dissociation all of these that give us a  little bit more information about the  molecules to help us and identify  differences in those molecules that are  separated with ion mobility  you  okay next question how can resolution be  improved in the case of diastereomers or  even enantiomers  that's a good question I think that's  really one of that that illustrates one  of the major advantages to using ion  mobility is for things like isomers  so with diastereomers when we have some  differences in the stereochemistry  we're largely able to separate a lot of  these and I've shown a number of  examples of them throughout the  presentation now in order to make those  kind of separations better I think that  goes back to the first question that I  answered which is in our ability to get  higher resolution measurements so that  very very subtle structural differences  between things like diastereomers and  epimers can still be resolved with this  now enantiomers are kind of an  interesting problem because enantiomers  or mirror images are essentially at the  exact same collision cross-section and  by themselves cannot be resolved with  ion mobility however we've published a  number of cases in which we can separate  those mostly by interacting them with  some other kind of molecule that has  chiral structure to it so we can get  these complexes where two different  enantiomers will interact with a chiral  molecule and can thus be separated using  ion mobility and I think that's really  the only way that it can be done  although it's important to note that  these approaches are far from universal  and can be technically very challenging  to develop so enantiomers are still  really what one might consider the holy  grail of ion mobility separations but  they can be done  once again thank Chris for his  presentation I'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today any  questions we do not have time for today  we'll be addressed by the speaker via  the contact information you provided at  the time of registration we would like  to thank laberd's and our sponsor  Agilent for underwriting today's  educational webcast laberd's will alert  you via email when it's available for  replay we encourage you to share that  email with your colleagues you may have  missed today's event until next time  goodbye
 

